# Shipped inside each uploaded site folder by scripts/deploy.sh.
#
# Hidden files. Ubuntu's stock Apache config denies only `.ht*`, so every other
# dotfile in a web root is served as plain text: a stray .env, a .git directory,
# an editor swap file, a .secrets someone copied in while debugging. Nothing is
# exposed today, but only because those files do not happen to be here — rsync
# --delete copies whatever is in out/, and "we didn't put one there" is not a
# rule. This is the rule. It lives here rather than in the shared root
# .htaccess so it ships with every deploy instead of waiting for someone to
# remember scripts/droplet-server-config.sh.
#
# /.well-known/ is exempt: ACME writes the certificate-renewal challenge under
# it, and a renewal that fails takes the site dark behind its own two-year
# HSTS. (The live challenge path is the shared web root's, above these folders,
# so this rule would not see it anyway — the exemption is here so that stays
# true if a site folder ever becomes a document root of its own.)
#
# One dotfile really is published here: .build-marker.json, written into out/
# by scripts/write-build-marker.mjs so the page guards know what they are
# grading. It is denied along with the rest, and that breaks nothing — the two
# suites that read it (tests/rendered-pages, tests/link-audit) read it off disk
# before the upload, never over HTTP, and version.txt is the file that says
# what is live to anyone asking from outside.
#
# 403, not 404: the match is on the URL shape, never on what is on disk, so it
# answers the same whether or not the file exists and tells an attacker nothing
# — the same way the stock `.ht*` rule already behaves.
#
# RedirectMatch rather than RewriteRule, deliberately: switching RewriteEngine
# on in a per-directory .htaccess makes Apache drop the parent's rewrite rules
# for this whole tree, which would silently kill the plain-http -> https
# redirect and the Pro shell redirects that live in the shared root block
# (scripts/droplet-htaccess.conf). mod_alias merges instead of replacing.
# The root block carries the same deny as a backstop, written as a rewrite
# because that file already runs a rewrite engine; the two are not a copy-paste
# to be tidied into one.
RedirectMatch 403 "(^|/)\.(?!well-known/)"

# Cache busting. CSS and JS under _next/static are named after their contents,
# so a changed file gets a new name; they're cached for a year (set higher up).
# Everything else here — the pages, data files, images — is "no-cache": the
# browser keeps a copy but checks for a newer one on every load, so a refresh
# after a deploy always shows it. Unchanged files answer 304, nothing re-downloads.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <If "%{REQUEST_URI} !~ m#/_next/static/#">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache"
  </If>
</IfModule>
